# Part Two — Today's Debates

> Topic 6 questions are almost all **debates**: quotas for or against, results or no results, preference or fairness. Each debate below gives you facts, both sides and a line to take.

## Debate 1 · Reserved seats: direct or indirect election?

Pakistan's 60 women's seats are filled from **party lists**, not by voters.
- **For indirect seats:** they guarantee women's presence; they bring educated professionals who might never win a constituency; they are simple to administer.
- **For direct election:** women would have constituencies, voters and legitimacy; they would be less dependent on party leaders; it would end the "nominated" stigma. Options include **rotating reserved constituencies** (as in India's panchayats) or **dual-member constituencies** with one woman member.
- **Line to take:** move gradually to **direct election** — the main weakness of Pakistan's quota is that it gives presence without a power base.

## Debate 2 · Is the 5% ticket rule meaningful?

The **Elections Act 2017** requires parties to give 5% of general-seat tickets to women.
- **Supporters:** it is the first candidate quota; more women contested in ==2018== and ==2024==; 11 women won general seats in 2024.
- **Critics:** 5% is tiny; parties place women in **losing** seats; there are no real sanctions.
- **Line to take:** raise it (to at least **10–33%**), require women in **winnable** seats, and attach sanctions.

## Debate 3 · Has the quota become a ceiling?

Since ==2002==, parties have tended to treat reserved seats as women's "share" and give men nearly all general tickets.
- **Line to take:** the quota should be a **floor**; candidate quotas and party reform are needed so that women compete for general seats too.

## Debate 4 · Dynastic women — empowerment or proxy?

Many women leaders — Benazir Bhutto, Maryam Nawaz, and many reserved-seat members — come from political families.
- **Empowerment view:** dynasties gave women a route into a closed system; many became leaders in their own right.
- **Proxy view:** dynasty keeps power in elite families and excludes ordinary women.
- **Line to take:** dynasty is a **route, not a disqualification**; the problem is the lack of **other** routes.

## Debate 5 · Do women representatives represent "women"?

Women differ by class, region, religion and party.
- **Line to take:** women legislators have united on **violence and rights** (the 2010–2016 laws), but poor, rural and minority women remain under-represented — an **intersectional** gap (Topic 5).

## Debate 6 · Should political participation be treated as a human right? (2024)

- **Yes:** **UDHR** Article 21 (==1948==), **ICCPR** Article 25 (==1966==), **CEDAW** Article 7 (==1979==) and Pakistan's **Articles 17, 25 and 34** protect the right to vote, stand and take part in public affairs.
- **What follows:** if participation is a right, the state must remove barriers — registration, violence, party discrimination — not just allow women to try.
- **Line to take:** participation is a **human right**; quotas and protective measures are ways of making it **real**.

## Debate 7 · After the 2025 "zero women ministers"

The **Global Gender Gap Report 2025** recorded no women in Pakistan's federal cabinet; the 2026 report showed political empowerment up 11 places (to ==107th==).
- **Line to take:** appointing women to cabinet is the **quickest, cheapest** way to raise women's political empowerment — but appointments can be reversed; elected women on general seats are more durable.
